\begin{frontmatter}

\title{Which representation clusters? A chance-corrected benchmark of ECG
representations for unsupervised arrhythmia structure}

\author[uh]{Dongho Chun}
\author[af]{Axel Faes\corref{cor1}}
\cortext[cor1]{Corresponding author.}
\ead{axel.faes@uhasselt.be}
\affiliation[uh]{organization={Data Science Institute (DSI), Hasselt University}, city={Hasselt}, country={Belgium}}
\affiliation[af]{organization={Biomedical Data Sciences, Data Science Institute (DSI) and Biomedical Research Institute (BIOMED), Hasselt University}, city={Hasselt}, country={Belgium}}

\begin{abstract}
Unsupervised analysis of the electrocardiogram (ECG) promises label-free
discovery of cardiac structure, yet whether clustering succeeds depends less on
the algorithm than on how the signal is represented. We benchmark four
representation families for unsupervised separation of sinus rhythm (SR) from
atrial fibrillation (AF) on PTB-XL: whole-recording raw flattening, a convolutional
reconstruction autoencoder, compact hand-crafted rhythm and morphology features,
and two frozen foundation-model embeddings (HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder).
Evaluation is chance-corrected (adjusted Rand index, ARI), patient-grouped,
\rev{repeated over ten seeds for the clusterers that depend on one}, and bounded by a
supervised ceiling. ECGFounder clusters
best (ARI up to 0.885), but 16 interpretable hand-crafted features are competitive
(0.711) and far ahead of the raw and autoencoder pipelines (ARI $\approx 0$), which
match a majority-class assignment despite supervised ceilings well above chance.
The same ordering holds on a MIT-BIH beat-morphology task. Two cautions emerge: the
silhouette score rewards the degenerate one-cluster solution (silhouette 0.81 at
ARI \rev{0.000}) and cannot validate ECG clustering alone, and the feature scaler materially
changes the outcome. The ranking is largely expected, since SR and AF differ by
definition in rhythm regularity; the contribution is the chance-corrected controls
that locate where naive pipelines fail and the finding that the cheap interpretable
representation is the more deployable one. Under heavy class imbalance and
federation across ten heterogeneous sites, the hand-crafted features are the most
robust, overtaking the foundation embedding under imbalance while the embedding's
centralized advantage erodes under federation. The representation, not the
algorithm, governs label-free ECG structure.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keyword}
electrocardiography \sep unsupervised clustering \sep representation learning \sep
foundation models \sep adjusted Rand index \sep PTB-XL
\end{keyword}

\end{frontmatter}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Most machine-learning work on the electrocardiogram (ECG) is supervised, but the
clinically interesting questions are often unsupervised: which patients group
together, what subtypes exist, and whether structure can be found without labels
that are expensive or unavailable. A natural first attempt is to cluster recordings
directly. Whether that attempt succeeds is usually attributed to the clustering
algorithm, yet the choice that dominates is the representation fed to it.

This study began as a re-examination of a master's project that clustered whole
12-lead recordings. That pipeline flattened each strip into a long vector, compressed
it with a dense autoencoder, and clustered the code. It reported high cluster purity
and a silhouette score near 0.89, but the purity simply equalled the majority-class
proportion in every setting, and the silhouette was measured on a degenerate solution
in which almost all points fell into a single cluster. The honest conclusion of that
work, that the difficulty lies in processing the raw signal rather than in the
clustering step, is correct and is the starting point here. We make it precise.

A representation can carry information about a label and still fail to cluster by it.
Linear separability and clusterability are distinct properties: a representation may
place the discriminative direction far from its dominant axes of variation, so that a
supervised probe recovers the label while an unsupervised partition does not. This
distinction is established in the self-supervised learning literature
\cite{sslclustering2025}, and we treat it as background rather than as a finding. What
has not been measured, for ECG, is how the common representation families compare on
this axis under proper chance-corrected metrics.

The most directly related work benchmarks several ECG foundation models against one
another, including a cluster-separability analysis on PTB-XL \cite{lookingbeyond2026}.
By its own statement it does not include a representation-wise comparison against
hand-crafted features, and it omits the MIT-BIH database. Those two gaps are exactly
where a careful, honest benchmark adds value, because hand-crafted features remain the
interpretable and inexpensive default in clinical signal processing, and MIT-BIH is the
canonical morphology benchmark.

We therefore benchmark four representation families for unsupervised SR-versus-AF
separation \rev{(sinus rhythm versus atrial fibrillation)}, with the controls the original
pipeline lacked. The resulting ranking is not
the contribution. Because SR and AF differ by definition in the regularity of the
rhythm, that compact hand-crafted features and a large foundation model both recover the
split is expected; what the benchmark adds is what the controls reveal and how the
representations behave under deployment stress. Our contributions are modest and
empirical. (i) A chance-corrected, patient-grouped, significance-tested clustering
benchmark of raw, reconstruction-autoencoder, hand-crafted, and two frozen
foundation-model representations, each bounded by a supervised ceiling and shown stable
across the assumed number of clusters. (ii) Two reproducible methodological cautions that
explain the original project's misleading numbers: internal validity indices reward the
degenerate collapse, and feature conditioning changes the result by a wide margin.
(iii) The deployment finding, under two independent stressors: with heavy class imbalance
and with federated non-independent client splits, the 16 hand-crafted features are the
most robust, \rev{overtaking the foundation embedding under imbalance and holding far more of
their clustering ARI under site heterogeneity}. (iv) A generalization check on a
MIT-BIH beat-morphology task, the dataset the closest prior benchmark omits. We do not
propose a new method, we scope every foundation-model statement to the two models we
test, and we credit the separability-versus-clusterability phenomenon to prior work.

\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Unsupervised ECG and representations.} Hand-crafted features built from
RR-interval irregularity and beat morphology encode the defining signatures of common
arrhythmias and underpin decades of automatic analysis \cite{hrvstandards1996,dechazal2004}.
Tensor and wavelet decompositions have been combined with spectral clustering for
unsupervised 12-lead classification \cite{wavelettensorecg2018}. Reconstruction
autoencoders optimise signal energy rather than class structure, which makes them
strong compressors but weak organisers of class geometry. Contrastive self-supervised
methods built on physiologically motivated augmentations and patient- or lead-level
invariances, including CLOCS \cite{clocs2021}, 3KG \cite{threekg2021}, and contrastive
predictive coding for 12-lead ECG \cite{mehari2022ssl}, established the pretraining
recipes that later foundation models scaled up. Self-supervised and foundation models for
ECG, such as HuBERT-ECG \cite{hubertecg2024} and ECGFounder \cite{ecgfounder2024}, learn
general representations from millions of recordings and are now evaluated as frozen
feature extractors. The wavelet-tensor and spectral-clustering pipeline of
\cite{wavelettensorecg2018} addresses a related unsupervised goal with a different
(tensor) representation; we focus on the four families above, which span the energy-only
to definition-encoding spectrum, and leave a tensor arm to future work.

\paragraph{Clustering evaluation.} Cluster purity is not chance-corrected: assigning
every point to the majority class attains a purity equal to that class's prevalence.
External, chance-corrected indices, the adjusted Rand index \cite{hubertarabie1985} and
adjusted mutual information \cite{vinh2010}, are near zero for such trivial solutions
and are the appropriate headline metrics. Internal indices such as the silhouette
\cite{rousseeuw1987} measure compactness in the embedding being clustered and can be
maximised by degenerate solutions, so they require cluster-size sanity checks.

\paragraph{Closest benchmark.} The ECG foundation-model benchmark of
\cite{lookingbeyond2026} reports cluster-separability (ARI) of foundation-model
embeddings on PTB-XL and finds that some models cluster by clinical label while others
cluster by dataset. It compares foundation models to one another and states that prior
work omits a representation-wise evaluation; it does not add a hand-crafted baseline,
and it does not include MIT-BIH. We fill both gaps and add a supervised ceiling so that
a low clustering score can be attributed to geometry rather than to missing information.

\section{Methods}\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Data and cohorts}

\paragraph{PTB-XL.} We use PTB-XL \cite{ptbxl2020}, a public 12-lead database
(21\,799 records, 18\,869 patients; 10\,s strips). Rhythm labels are taken from the
SCP-ECG \rev{(Standard Communications Protocol for Computer-Assisted Electrocardiography)}
statements. The SR-versus-AF cohort uses all records carrying an AF statement
(AFIB or AFLT) as positives and sinus-rhythm records without any AF statement as
controls. PTB-XL encodes rhythm statements at a likelihood of zero meaning ``present'',
so a presence-based rule is required; a confidence threshold would discard every SR
record. We draw a balanced, patient-disjoint cohort of 2\,542 records (1\,282 SR,
1\,260 AF), so the majority-class baseline is 0.504. \rev{Records are read from the
\mbox{100\,Hz} distribution of the database with \texttt{wfdb.rdsamp} and cast once to a
\texttt{float32} array of 1000 samples by 12 leads. No filtering, resampling or rescaling
is applied at load time, so every representation starts from the same array and declares
its own preprocessing. The lead set is a per-representation choice rather than one setting
for the whole study, and is stated with each representation below.} All evaluation is
patient-grouped: no patient appears in more than one fold or in both classes.

\paragraph{MIT-BIH.} For generalization we use the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database
\cite{mitbih2001,physionet2000}. Each annotated beat is segmented into a window of
$[-300, +250]$\,ms around the R-peak across both leads. We form a balanced
normal-versus-premature-ventricular (N-vs-V) task of 3\,000 beats (1\,500 per class)
from 47 records, with the record as the grouping unit. This is a morphology task and a
deliberate contrast to the PTB-XL rhythm task.

\subsection{Representations}

We compare four families on PTB-XL (Table~\ref{tab:main}). \rev{Each entry states the
leads it consumes, the array it receives, and the principle it encodes.}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Raw flattening} reproduces the original pipeline: each lead is
moving-average downsampled and all leads are concatenated into one vector
($\sim$2000 dimensions). It carries amplitude and energy but no cross-recording
temporal alignment. \rev{Input: all twelve leads, \texttt{float32}, 1000 by 12. A
non-overlapping six-sample mean per lead leaves 166 samples per lead and a
1992-dimensional vector. The principle is that the sample amplitudes are themselves the
features, with no clinical model imposed on the signal, and all twelve leads enter because
this arm reproduces the originating pipeline, which concatenated every lead.}
\item \textbf{Convolutional autoencoder} (conv-AE): a 1-D convolutional autoencoder
with a 32-unit bottleneck, trained to convergence with a validation split. This is a
properly specified reconstruction model, in contrast to the original collapsed dense
autoencoder. \rev{Input: all twelve leads, \texttt{float32}, resampled to 12 by 2500 and
z-scored per lead. The principle is that a reconstruction objective compresses whatever
carries signal energy, which need not be what separates the classes.}
\item \textbf{Hand-crafted} (16 features): RR-interval statistics and irregularity
\rev{(the standard deviation of the RR intervals, SDNN; the root mean square of successive
RR differences, RMSSD; the proportion of successive RR differences above \mbox{50\,ms},
pNN50; the coefficient of variation, RR entropy and mean heart rate)}, P-wave
presence and amplitude, QRS width and amplitude, and beat-template consistency
\cite{hrvstandards1996,dechazal2004}, extracted with NeuroKit2 \cite{neurokit2021}.
\rev{Input: lead II alone, \texttt{float32}, 1000 samples, reduced to a 16-dimensional
\texttt{float32} vector. R-peak detection and wave delineation run on that one lead. Lead
II is the standard rhythm lead, the one on which RR regularity and P-wave presence are
read, single-lead recordings are the established setting for automated AF detection
\cite{clifford2017af}, and a single-lead definition is what allows the same feature logic
to be carried to the two-lead MIT-BIH beats, where a twelve-lead definition would not
apply. The principle is that the AF signature is RR irregularity together with P-wave
absence, so the representation measures those two quantities directly.}
\item \textbf{Foundation embeddings} (frozen): mean-pooled hidden states of HuBERT-ECG
(768-d) \cite{hubertecg2024} and the 1024-d pooled features of ECGFounder
\cite{ecgfounder2024}, both used as fixed feature extractors with no fine-tuning.
\rev{Input: all twelve leads, \texttt{float32}. HuBERT-ECG receives the first \mbox{5\,s}
at \mbox{100\,Hz}, flattened lead-major into a 6000-sample vector; ECGFounder receives the
full strip resampled to 12 by 5000 for its \mbox{500\,Hz} input and z-scored once over the
whole array. Both lead sets are fixed by the published models, which are defined on the
complete twelve-lead strip. The principle is that large-scale pretraining has already
fixed an ECG feature space, and the question is whether that space places the rhythm
classes in separate regions.}
\end{itemize}
On MIT-BIH the rhythm features do not transfer to single beats, so we compare a raw
beat window, a 21-dimensional beat-morphology and RR-context feature set, and the
conv-AE. The 12-lead foundation models are not applied to 2-lead beats; this scoping
limit is itself informative, because such models are tied to the 12-lead strip format.

\rev{The two lead sets are deliberate and answer to different constraints. The hand-crafted
arm is single-lead by clinical convention and so that the same features transfer to the
two-lead beats; the raw arm is twelve-lead because that is the pipeline it reproduces. The
asymmetry gives the raw arm more input than the hand-crafted arm, not less, so the lead set
cannot account for the raw arm's chance-level clustering.}

\subsection{Clustering and evaluation}

Each representation is clustered with $k$-means, Ward agglomerative, spectral,
\rev{a Gaussian mixture model (GMM) and hierarchical density-based spatial clustering of
applications with noise (HDBSCAN)}, with the number of clusters set to the number of classes
\cite{sklearn2011}. \rev{The implementations are those of scikit-learn, run with ten
$k$-means restarts per seed, Ward linkage on Euclidean distance, a nearest-neighbour
affinity with $k$-means label assignment for spectral clustering, diagonal covariance with
a covariance floor of \mbox{$10^{-4}$} for the Gaussian mixture, since a full covariance is
singular for the high-dimensional representations, and a minimum cluster size of $n/50$
rounded down and floored at 10 for HDBSCAN \cite{hdbscan2013}, which is 50 records on the
PTB-XL cohort. Tolerances and iteration caps are the library defaults, and no early
stopping is imposed on the clusterers. Ward agglomerative and HDBSCAN are deterministic
given the input matrix, so each is fitted once rather than once per seed. The conv-AE is
trained with Adam at a learning rate of \mbox{$10^{-3}$}, batch size 64, for at most 50
epochs on a 90/10 train/validation split, stopping when the validation reconstruction error
has not improved for eight epochs and restoring the best-validation weights. The two
foundation models are used in evaluation mode with gradients disabled and their published
weights unchanged: HuBERT-ECG is mean-pooled over time from its last hidden layer at batch
size 64, and ECGFounder returns its globally average-pooled 1024-dimensional feature layer
at batch size 32. Conditioning is fitted on the full representation matrix, and any
hand-crafted feature a record leaves undefined is filled with that feature's cohort median
before conditioning.} The headline metrics are the chance-corrected adjusted Rand index
(ARI) \cite{hubertarabie1985} and adjusted mutual information \rev{(AMI)} \cite{vinh2010};
normalized mutual information \rev{(NMI)}, V-measure, Fowlkes-Mallows, purity, silhouette
\cite{rousseeuw1987}, and the largest-cluster fraction are reported alongside. Every
metric is computed against a majority and a random baseline. \rev{Table~\ref{tab:metrics}
gives each reported metric with its definition, its range, and how a value on it is read.
There $U$ is the partition by true class and $V$ the partition by cluster, $n$ is the
number of records and $n_{ij}$ the number of records in class $i$ and cluster $j$, RI is
the fraction of record pairs on which $U$ and $V$ agree, $I$ and $H$ are mutual information
and entropy with $\bar{H}$ the arithmetic mean of $H(U)$ and $H(V)$, $E[\cdot]$ is the
expectation under the permutation model that holds both sets of group sizes fixed, and
$a_k$ and $b_k$ are the mean distance from record $k$ to the rest of its own cluster and to
the nearest other cluster. None of the eight carries a physical unit: the first five are
dimensionless indices of agreement between the true partition and the recovered one, purity
is the majority-class share inside the clusters, the silhouette is a dimensionless index of
cluster compactness computed without labels, and the largest-cluster fraction is a
proportion of the cohort.} A supervised ceiling bounds how much of the
label a probe recovers from the same features under the same patient-grouped five-fold
protocol; we report both a linear probe (balanced logistic regression) and a non-linear
probe (random forest), so that a high ceiling with a low ARI identifies a geometric
rather than an informational failure. Each configuration is repeated over ten seeds, and
the headline contrasts are tested with a paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test across seeds.
Because heavy-tailed features can dominate Euclidean distance, we sweep four conditioners
(Yeo-Johnson power, standard, quantile, robust) and report power scaling as the default
unless stated otherwise. To check that fixing the cluster count to the number of classes
does not flatter the comparison, we also report adjusted mutual information, which is
comparable across cluster counts, for $K$ from 2 to 8. Because the natural SR-to-AF
prevalence is imbalanced, we additionally evaluate on a 9:1 SR:AF subsample.

\begin{table}[!ht]
\centering
\caption[Reported clustering metrics.]{\rev{Reported clustering metrics: definition, range
and how a value is read. The notation is defined in the text.}}
\label{tab:metrics}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcp{0.36\linewidth}@{}}
\toprule
Metric & Definition & Range & Reading \\
\midrule
ARI & $\dfrac{\mathrm{RI}-E[\mathrm{RI}]}{\max\mathrm{RI}-E[\mathrm{RI}]}$ & $\le 1$ & Chance-corrected pair agreement. 0 at chance, 1 for identical partitions. \\[4pt]
AMI & $\dfrac{I-E[I]}{\bar{H}-E[I]}$ & $\le 1$ & Chance-corrected shared information. 0 at chance, 1 for identical partitions. \\[4pt]
NMI & $I/\bar{H}$ & $[0,1]$ & Shared information without the chance correction; rises with the cluster count. \\
V-measure & $2hc/(h+c)$ & $[0,1]$ & Harmonic mean of homogeneity $h$ and completeness $c$; equals NMI under arithmetic normalization. \\
Fowlkes-Mallows & $\sqrt{PR}$ & $[0,1]$ & Geometric mean of pair precision $P$ and pair recall $R$; not chance-corrected. \\
purity & $\frac{1}{n}\sum_j \max_i n_{ij}$ & $[0,1]$ & Majority-class share inside clusters. A single cluster already scores 0.504 here. \\
silhouette & $\frac{1}{n}\sum_k \dfrac{b_k-a_k}{\max(a_k,b_k)}$ & $[-1,1]$ & Internal and label-free. 1 tight and separated, 0 overlapping, negative misassigned. \\[4pt]
largest-cluster frac. & $\max_j |v_j|/n$ & $[1/|V|,1]$ & Degeneracy check. 1 means one cluster holds every record. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\rev{HDBSCAN labels records it does not assign to any dense region as noise, and the
metrics treat that label in two ways. ARI, AMI, NMI, V-measure and Fowlkes-Mallows receive
the prediction unchanged, so the noise records form one further group of their own and are
charged against the agreement with the true partition. Purity and the silhouette are
computed on the assigned records only, because neither is defined for a set that is not a
cluster, and the purity denominator stays the full cohort. Noise records are never dropped
from the evaluation and never reassigned to a nearest cluster. This is why HDBSCAN sits at
the bottom of every representation: at this minimum cluster size it assigns a minority of
the cohort on the representations that cluster well, and on the raw, conv-AE and HuBERT-ECG
matrices it labels the entire cohort as noise, which leaves one group and an ARI of exactly
zero.}

\rev{Uncertainty is quantified at two levels, which answer different questions. A
percentile bootstrap over the ten per-seed values, \mbox{10\,000} resamples, measures how
far a result moves when only the random seed moves. These intervals are narrow: across the
five PTB-XL representations the $k$-means seed interval spans less than 0.002 in ARI, and
for two of them it has zero width because every seed returns the same partition. Ward
agglomerative and HDBSCAN have no seed interval at all, since each is fitted once. A seed
interval therefore carries no more information than the seed standard deviation it would
replace. The interval reported with the results is the second one, a patient-grouped
percentile bootstrap over the evaluation cohort with \mbox{5\,000} resamples: the
partitions are held fixed and the cohort is resampled with replacement at the patient
level, so every record of a drawn patient travels with it. The statistic resampled is the
quantity the tables report, the mean ARI over the ten seeds where the clusterer is seeded and
the single fit where it is not, so each interval contains its own point estimate. All
intervals are the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the bootstrap distribution at a fixed
bootstrap seed.}

\rev{Each paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test is reported with two effect sizes. With ten
paired seeds the two-sided p-value is pinned at its floor of 0.002 as soon as every seed
moves the same way, so it separates none of the contrasts and cannot express a magnitude.
Cliff's delta gives the dominance of one arm over the other across all cross-seed pairs, on
a scale from $-1$ to $1$, and the matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation rescales the
signed-rank statistic itself onto the same scale. Each headline contrast additionally
carries the paired difference in ARI with its own patient-grouped bootstrap interval.}

\subsection{Federated robustness}

To probe deployment under data siloing we use standard federated $k$-means with
FedAvg-style centroid aggregation \cite{fedavg2017}; we do not propose a new federated
method. The 2\,542 records are partitioned, patient-grouped, across ten simulated sites,
either independently and identically distributed (IID) or with class proportions drawn
from a Dirichlet distribution so that sites differ sharply in arrhythmia prevalence
(non-IID). \rev{The Dirichlet concentration is 0.3, applied per class over the ten sites,
and any site left holding fewer records than clusters is dropped. Conditioning is fitted
once on the full representation matrix before partitioning, so the federation concerns the
clustering step alone. A run is fifteen aggregation rounds of three local Lloyd iterations
per site, with the server replacing each centroid by the mean of the site centroids
weighted by the number of records each site assigned to it, and the centroids initialised
globally from two randomly drawn records. Both settings are repeated over ten seeds, with
the site partition and the centroid initialisation redrawn at each seed.} We report the
global ARI relative to centralized clustering of the same representation.

\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Representation, not algorithm, governs clusterability}

Table~\ref{tab:main} and Fig.~\ref{fig:heatmap} summarise the PTB-XL benchmark.
ECGFounder clusters best (ARI up to 0.885 with $k$-means), the hand-crafted features
are competitive (0.711 with agglomerative), HuBERT-ECG is weak (0.195), and the raw and
conv-AE pipelines are indistinguishable from a majority-class assignment (ARI
$\approx 0$). The spread across clustering algorithms within a strong representation is
smaller than the spread across representations, so the representation is the deciding
factor.

