\rev{Uncertainty enters at two levels and they are of different size. Moving only the seed
leaves the $k$-means partitions almost unchanged, so the spread reported with the results is the
patient-grouped cohort bootstrap given in the caption of Table~\ref{tab:main}, whose intervals
separate every pair of representations except raw-flatten from conv-AE.} A paired Wilcoxon
signed-rank test on the $k$-means seeds confirms every adjacent contrast, ECGFounder over
hand-crafted ($\Delta = 0.242$\rev{, paired bootstrap interval [0.202, 0.280]}), hand-crafted
over HuBERT-ECG ($\Delta = 0.470$\rev{, [0.424, 0.515]}), and HuBERT-ECG over raw ($\Delta =
0.174$\rev{, [0.146, 0.203]}), at $p = 0.002$, the floor for ten paired samples. \rev{That floor
is reached by any contrast in which all ten seeds move the same way, so the p-value orders none
of the three and each carries two effect sizes as well. Cliff's delta and the matched-pairs
rank-biserial correlation are both 1.00 on all three: every cross-seed pair and every signed
rank favours the higher-ranked representation. The magnitudes are therefore read from the paired
differences and their intervals above.} The ordering does not depend on fixing the cluster count
to the number of classes: \rev{with adjusted mutual information for $K$ from 2 to 8, ECGFounder
leads at every $K$, from 0.825 at $K=2$ to a minimum of 0.441 at $K=7$ and 0.450 at $K=8$;
the hand-crafted features fall monotonically from 0.557 to 0.329; HuBERT-ECG stays at or
below 0.132 and the raw pipeline never exceeds 0.005}.

The two foundation models do not behave as one category. ECGFounder clusters strongly while
HuBERT-ECG does not, which matches the report of \cite{lookingbeyond2026} that some ECG
foundation embeddings organise by clinical label and others by dataset. We therefore name
each model and do not generalise to foundation models as a class. HDBSCAN labels most
points as noise and underperforms throughout, and spectral clustering collapses where the
nearest-neighbour affinity graph is poorly connected; we report both rather than tune them
away.


\rev{Each representation's position follows from two things: how much of the label its space
holds, which the supervised ceiling in the last column of Table~\ref{tab:main} measures, and
where in the space that label lies. The hand-crafted representation is 16-dimensional and each
coordinate is a quantity by which the classes are defined, RR irregularity and P-wave presence;
its ceiling is 0.942 and an unsupervised partition recovers 0.711. ECGFounder has the highest
ceiling, 0.983, recovers the most, 0.885, and leads at every $K$ from 2 to 8, so the rhythm
split is one of the larger sources of variation in its 1024 dimensions. HuBERT-ECG has almost
the same ceiling as the hand-crafted features, 0.899, and recovers 0.195. The conv-AE ceiling of
0.678 is what a bottleneck optimised to reconstruct signal energy retains of a class it was
never asked about, and it recovers 0.008. Raw flattening reaches 0.519 against a majority of
0.504, so a linear probe finds almost no label in the 1992-dimensional vector at all: its
coordinates are amplitudes at fixed sample offsets, and no fixed offset falls on the same part
of the cardiac cycle in two recordings. The raw and conv-AE rows both sit at a majority-class
assignment under all five clusterers, and they do so for different reasons, one for want of
label information in the space and one for want of any arrangement of it, so changing the
clustering algorithm addresses neither. Which of the two accounts for HuBERT-ECG is what the
next subsection measures.}

\subsection{Separability is not clusterability}

The supervised ceilings (last column of Table~\ref{tab:main}; Fig.~\ref{fig:sep})
expose the gap. The raw pipeline carries almost no label information (ceiling 0.519,
barely above the 0.504 majority), consistent with its chance-level clustering. The
conv-AE is more separable (0.678) yet still clusters at chance (\rev{best ARI 0.008, from Ward
agglomerative}): a properly
trained reconstruction autoencoder encodes the class without organising it into
clusters. HuBERT-ECG is the sharpest case, with a ceiling of 0.899 but an ARI of only
0.195. ECGFounder and the hand-crafted features achieve both high separability and high
clusterability. The discriminative information exists in the weaker embeddings; it
simply does not lie along their dominant axes of variation, the established
separability-versus-clusterability effect \cite{sslclustering2025}. This is not an
artefact of a linear probe: a random forest on the raw features reaches accuracy \rev{0.714},
well above its linear ceiling of \rev{0.519} and far above its clustering ARI of \rev{0.000}, so even a
non-linear decoder recovers a label that clustering does not. \rev{The same probe gives 0.951
on the hand-crafted features, 0.983 on ECGFounder and 0.825 on HuBERT-ECG. On ECGFounder the two
probes agree to three decimals, the linear ceiling at 0.9831 and the random forest at 0.9835.
For HuBERT-ECG the random forest sits below the linear ceiling of 0.899, so probe linearity
does not account for that gap either.}


\rev{That reading is testable and we test it out of fold. A linear discriminant is fitted on the
training patients of a patient-grouped stratified five-fold split, the held-out patients are
projected onto that single axis, and the projection is clustered with $k$-means without labels;
the comparison arm is $k$-means on the same held-out records in their native representation.
Fitting the discriminant on every label and then clustering the projection would recover the
labels by construction, which is why the projected patients are held out. Over five folds and
ten seeds, on 508 or 509 held-out records per fold, the mean ARI rises from 0.160 to 0.549 for
HuBERT-ECG, from 0.003 to 0.121 for the conv-AE, from 0.646 to 0.758 for the hand-crafted
features and from 0.893 to 0.923 for ECGFounder, and does not move for raw flattening, 0.000 to
0.001. These are held-out values, so the native column does not equal the full-cohort $k$-means
ARIs of Table~\ref{tab:main}; the largest difference is 0.013, on HuBERT-ECG. HuBERT-ECG is
where the two readings separate furthest: one direction estimated from other patients recovers
more than three times what its own 768 dimensions yield unsupervised, so the class information
is present, transfers across patients, and lies away from the directions that dominate the
embedding. ECGFounder gains almost nothing, because its dominant axes already carry the split.
The conv-AE rises off chance and stops far short of its ceiling of 0.678, so its class structure
is both off-axis and diffuse. Raw flattening does not move at all, which separates its failure
from HuBERT-ECG's: no single direction estimated from other patients separates the classes in
it, and whatever the non-linear probe recovers from it is not of that form.}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.60\linewidth]{figures/fig3_sep_vs_clust.pdf}
\caption{Separability is not clusterability. Supervised ceiling against clustering
ARI per representation\rev{, on the cohort of 2\,542 records}; points far below parity are
separable but not clusterable. \rev{All five clusterers are plotted for each representation.
The filled marker is that representation's best clusterer and carries its 95\%
patient-grouped bootstrap interval; the four open markers are the remaining clusterers,
offset slightly along the ceiling axis so that they do not cover the interval bar. The
dashed line is parity between the ceiling and the ARI.}}
\label{fig:sep}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The silhouette trap}

Internal indices mislead here in a specific, reproducible way. Raw flattening with
agglomerative clustering attains a silhouette of 0.81 while every point but a handful
falls into one cluster (largest-cluster fraction 1.00) and the ARI is 0.000. The
hand-crafted representation, which actually recovers the classes (ARI 0.711, balanced
clusters), has a \emph{lower} silhouette of 0.28 (Fig.~\ref{fig:trap}). A degenerate
one-cluster solution maximises compactness while carrying no class information. This is
the artefact behind the original project's silhouette of 0.89, and it is why
chance-corrected external metrics, read together with cluster-size sanity checks, are
necessary.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\linewidth]{figures/fig2_silhouette_trap.pdf}
\caption{The silhouette trap. Internal silhouette score against chance-corrected ARI;
marker size is the largest-cluster fraction. The degenerate one-cluster solutions
\rev{(large markers, lower right)} attain high silhouette at zero ARI. \rev{The cohort of
2\,542 records at power scaling, one point per seed for $k$-means, spectral clustering and
the Gaussian mixture and one point for each deterministic Ward and HDBSCAN fit: 157 points
over 22 of the 25 representation-by-clusterer cells. The three HDBSCAN fits that label the
whole cohort as noise leave the silhouette undefined and are not plotted. Each point is a
single clustering solution, so the panel carries no interval.}}
\label{fig:trap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Conditioning matters}

The feature scaler changes the outcome materially. For the hand-crafted features the
mean ARI over clusterers is \rev{0.629} under standard scaling and \rev{0.606} under power scaling, but
falls to \rev{0.360} under robust scaling and \rev{0.229} under quantile scaling. The effect is not
confined to hand-crafted features: ECGFounder's mean ARI drops from \rev{0.866} (standard) to
\rev{0.093} under robust scaling. Robust scaling, a common default for physiological signals,
leaves heavy arrhythmic tails unbounded so that a few outliers dominate Euclidean
distance and the partition collapses. Conditioning should be reported and swept, not
assumed.

\subsection{Generalization to MIT-BIH beat morphology}

The ordering replicates on a different dataset and a different task type
(Table~\ref{tab:mitbih}). On the N-vs-V beat-morphology task the 21-dimensional
hand-crafted features cluster well (ARI \rev{0.783}), whereas the raw beat window does not
(\rev{ARI 0.018 with $k$-means and 0.049 with agglomerative clustering}) despite beats being R-peak aligned. The conv-AE is again the clean
separability-versus-clusterability case: a supervised ceiling of \rev{0.927} but a best ARI of only
\rev{0.127, from the Gaussian mixture}. That the same pattern appears for rhythm on PTB-XL
and for morphology on MIT-BIH supports the representation-level reading.

